; SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
;
; varek_container: the SELinux rules VAREK's container needs (v1.32, D5
; in docs/security/v1.32-containers.md), for Podman (or Docker) on RHEL,
; Fedora and others with SELinux enforcing.
;
; The container runs as container_init_t, the distribution's own type for
; containers that manage their control groups (Podman uses it for systemd
; in a container). container-selinux (container.te) gives it what
; container_t has and more: cgroup_t directories and files managed, as
; sub-agents and confined MCP servers need (v1.28.1, v1.31); mounting on and
; unmounting the cgroup filesystem, sysfs and /proc, and remounting /proc and
; device_t filesystems, as systemd does; unmounting tmpfs; and the network
; unconfined (corenet_unconfined). Other containers keep container_t; this
; is not the host-wide container_manage_cgroup boolean.
;
; The four rules below, each measured refused without it on RHEL 9.8:
;   1. the cgroup filesystem remounted: the runtime mounts the container's
;      read-only, and the Warden bind-remounts it read-write in its own mount
;      namespace (its own groups only: --cgroupns=private); SELinux asks for
;      the filesystem's remount permission for any remount;
;   2. a mount on any of the container's own character devices
;      (container_file_t): the noexec mount of /dev/null, which a policy
;      letting the agent write it has the Warden bind onto itself (v1.27.1);
;   3. and 4. rootless (D10), where Podman binds the host's own device nodes
;      onto /dev/null (null_device_t) and /dev/tty (devtty_t): the same mount.
;
; On the host, as root:
;
;   semodule -i varek_container.cil
;
; and run the container with --security-opt label=type:container_init_t,
; with the settings in packaging/container/README.md.

(allow container_init_t cgroup_t (filesystem (remount)))
(allow container_init_t container_file_t (chr_file (mounton)))
(allow container_init_t null_device_t (chr_file (mounton)))
(allow container_init_t devtty_t (chr_file (mounton)))
